IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hoq-common`

-- Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (Queensland) -- what follows a complaint: investigation (ss 84, 85), what the complainant
-- must be told (ss 35K(3), 71, 90F, 95, 145(4), 278), conciliation (ss 140, 149), and the protections and claims of
-- part 15 division 6 and part 17 (ss 232, 261, 263, 263A, 264, 275).
--
-- SEAT: the person who makes a health service complaint (the complainant), and, for ss 232, 261-263A and 275, the
-- person who gives information or is harmed for complaining. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Ombudsman Act 2013: investigation, conciliation and protections`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 8 -- investigation of my complaint (ss 84, 85)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 85(1) "The health ombudsman must complete an investigation as quickly as is reasonable in all the circumstances
-- and, in any case, by the day (the due day) that is 1 year, or any extended time decided under subsection (2), after
-- the decision to carry out the investigation." (2) the ombudsman may extend the due day if it reasonably considers it
-- not possible to complete properly by then. (3) "The due day ... may be extended more than once ... but each
-- extension may not be more than 3 months." (9) "This section does not apply to an investigation carried out in
-- compliance with a direction by the Minister under section 81 if the direction includes a stated time by which the
-- investigation must be completed."
-- READING (fork F2, F8): 1 year is 12 months on; each extension runs on from the due day then current, in whole months,
-- and an extension stated as more than 3 months counts as 3.
GIVEN `the due day so far` IS A DATE
      `the extensions in months` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`s 85(3) -- the due day after the extensions` `the due day so far` `the extensions in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the extensions in months`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN `the due day so far`
    WHEN `e` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` THEN `s 85(3) -- the due day after the extensions` ((`the smaller of` `e` `and` 3) `months after` `the due day so far`) `the rest`

DECLARE `Investigation due day` IS ONE OF
    `the investigation must be completed by` HAS `the investigation due day` IS A DATE
    `s 85 does not apply: the Minister's direction to investigate states the time for completing it (s 85(9))`

@export By what day must the health ombudsman complete the investigation of my complaint (s 85)?
GIVEN `the day it decided to investigate` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman decide to investigate?
      `the extensions in months` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc How many months was each extension of the due day? (List them in order, or give none; each counts for at most 3 months.)
      `the Minister directed it with a stated time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the investigation being done because the Minister directed it, with a stated time to finish?
GIVETH A `Investigation due day`
`By when must the investigation be completed question` `the day it decided to investigate` `the extensions in months` `the Minister directed it with a stated time` MEANS
    IF `the Minister directed it with a stated time`
    THEN `s 85 does not apply: the Minister's direction to investigate states the time for completing it (s 85(9))`
    ELSE `the investigation must be completed by` (`s 85(3) -- the due day after the extensions` (12 `months after` `the day it decided to investigate`) `the extensions in months`)

-- s 85(4) "The health ombudsman must keep a register, on a publicly accessible website ..., of investigations that have
-- not been completed within 1 year after the decision to carry them out." (6) "The register must not include
-- information that identifies a complainant, health service provider or individual to whom a health service was
-- provided." (8) "If an investigation is not completed within 2 years after the decision to carry it out-- (a) the
-- health ombudsman must give notices to the Minister and the parliamentary committee stating-- (i) details of the
-- matter being investigated; and (ii) why the investigation has not been completed ...".
-- READING (fork F9): "within 1 year after" includes the day 12 months on.
DECLARE `A long investigation` IS ONE OF
    `s 85 does not apply: the Minister's direction states the time (s 85(9))`
    `nothing follows yet: it was completed, or is still within, 1 year after the decision`
    `it must be listed on the ombudsman's public register of investigations not completed within 1 year, without anything that identifies you (s 85(4)-(6))`
    `it must be on the public register, and the Minister and the parliamentary committee must be told what it is about and why it is not complete (s 85(4), (8))`

@export My complaint's investigation is taking a long time: must it be on the health ombudsman's public register of late investigations, and must the Minister and Parliament's committee be told (s 85(4)-(8))?
GIVEN `the day it decided to investigate` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman decide to investigate?
      `it is complete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the investigation been completed?
      `the day it was completed` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date?
      `the Minister directed it with a stated time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the investigation being done because the Minister directed it, with a stated time to finish?
GIVETH A `A long investigation`
`What follows from a long investigation question` `the day it decided to investigate` `it is complete` `the day it was completed` `today` `the Minister directed it with a stated time` MEANS
    IF `the Minister directed it with a stated time`
    THEN `s 85 does not apply: the Minister's direction states the time (s 85(9))`
    ELSE IF `the day to look at` `is on or before` (12 `months after` `the day it decided to investigate`)
         THEN `nothing follows yet: it was completed, or is still within, 1 year after the decision`
         ELSE IF `the day to look at` `is on or before` (24 `months after` `the day it decided to investigate`)
              THEN `it must be listed on the ombudsman's public register of investigations not completed within 1 year, without anything that identifies you (s 85(4)-(6))`
              ELSE `it must be on the public register, and the Minister and the parliamentary committee must be told what it is about and why it is not complete (s 85(4), (8))`
    WHERE
        `the day to look at` MEANS IF `it is complete` THEN `the day it was completed` ELSE `today`

-- s 84(1) "The health ombudsman must, at not less than 3 monthly intervals, give a notice of the progress of an
-- investigation to-- ... (b) if the investigation relates to a health service complaint, the complainant." (2) "A
-- person who has a right to be given a notice under subsection (1) may, by notice to the health ombudsman, waive the
-- right." (3) a waiver may be withdrawn before the investigation is complete.
-- READING (fork F10, finding 3): "at not less than 3 monthly intervals" is read as s 17(b) explains the scheme, a
-- report at least every 3 months; read literally it sets a minimum gap, not a maximum.
DECLARE `Next progress report` IS ONE OF
    `the next progress report is due by` HAS `the progress report due day` IS A DATE
    `none is owed to you: the investigation does not relate to your complaint (s 84(1)(b))`
    `none is owed to you while your waiver stands (s 84(2)); you may withdraw it before the investigation is complete (s 84(3))`
    `none is owed: the investigation is complete`

@export By what day must I get the next progress report on the investigation of my complaint (s 84)?
GIVEN `it relates to my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the investigation about the matter of your complaint?
      `I waived the reports` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you told the ombudsman, by notice, that you do not want progress reports (and not withdrawn that)?
      `it is complete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the investigation been completed?
      `the day it decided to investigate` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman decide to investigate?
      `I have had a progress report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had a progress report already?
      `the day of the last progress report` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was the last one given? (Any date if not.)
GIVETH A `Next progress report`
`By when must I get the next progress report question` `it relates to my complaint` `I waived the reports` `it is complete` `the day it decided to investigate` `I have had a progress report` `the day of the last progress report` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it relates to my complaint`)
    THEN `none is owed to you: the investigation does not relate to your complaint (s 84(1)(b))`
    ELSE IF `it is complete`
         THEN `none is owed: the investigation is complete`
         ELSE IF `I waived the reports`
              THEN `none is owed to you while your waiver stands (s 84(2)); you may withdraw it before the investigation is complete (s 84(3))`
              ELSE `the next progress report is due by` (3 `months after` (IF `I have had a progress report` THEN `the day of the last progress report` ELSE `the day it decided to investigate`))

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `What must I be told (ss 35K(3), 71, 90F, 95, 145(4), 278)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Something that happened` IS ONE OF
    `the ombudsman made a decision on my complaint (to act, take no further action, or not accept it)`
    `the complaint was referred to Ahpra at its request after the ombudsman proposed no further action`
    `an interim prohibition order was issued to the practitioner`
    `a prohibition order was issued to the practitioner`
    `the practitioner's registration was suspended or conditioned as immediate action`
    `a matter is before QCAT`
    `the conciliation of my complaint ended`

DECLARE `Must I be told` IS ONE OF
    `yes: notice of the decision, the action to be taken and the reasons, within 5 business days (s 278)`
    `no: you waived notice of decisions (s 278(4))`
    `yes: notice of the referral to Ahpra (s 35K(3))`
    `yes: notice of the details of the order that apply to the practitioner (ss 71, 90F)`
    `no: ss 71 and 90F require it only when the order was issued in response to a complaint`
    `the Act does not require you to be told of immediate registration action (s 279 requires notice to the practitioner's employers); the decision on your complaint is notified under s 278`
    `yes: you are a person QCAT notices must be given to, and notices the practitioner would give you come from the ombudsman or the National Board (s 95)`
    `no: s 95 applies only to a matter before QCAT arising from a health service complaint`
    `yes: a copy of the conciliator's results report, with any agreement reached (s 145(2), (4))`
    `the Act does not say on these facts`

@export Must I be told when this happens on my complaint, and what must I be given (ss 35K(3), 71, 90F, 95, 145(4), 278)?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `Something that happened` @desc What happened?
      `it arose from my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did it happen in response to, or arise from, your complaint?
      `I waived notice of decisions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you tell the ombudsman you did not need notice of its decisions on your complaint?
GIVETH A `Must I be told`
`Must I be told question` `what happened` `it arose from my complaint` `I waived notice of decisions` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it arose from my complaint`)
    THEN CONSIDER `what happened`
         WHEN `an interim prohibition order was issued to the practitioner` THEN `no: ss 71 and 90F require it only when the order was issued in response to a complaint`
         WHEN `a prohibition order was issued to the practitioner` THEN `no: ss 71 and 90F require it only when the order was issued in response to a complaint`
         WHEN `a matter is before QCAT` THEN `no: s 95 applies only to a matter before QCAT arising from a health service complaint`
         OTHERWISE `the Act does not say on these facts`
    ELSE CONSIDER `what happened`
         WHEN `the ombudsman made a decision on my complaint (to act, take no further action, or not accept it)` THEN
             IF `I waived notice of decisions`
             THEN `no: you waived notice of decisions (s 278(4))`
             ELSE `yes: notice of the decision, the action to be taken and the reasons, within 5 business days (s 278)`
         WHEN `the complaint was referred to Ahpra at its request after the ombudsman proposed no further action` THEN `yes: notice of the referral to Ahpra (s 35K(3))`
         WHEN `an interim prohibition order was issued to the practitioner` THEN `yes: notice of the details of the order that apply to the practitioner (ss 71, 90F)`
         WHEN `a prohibition order was issued to the practitioner` THEN `yes: notice of the details of the order that apply to the practitioner (ss 71, 90F)`
         WHEN `the practitioner's registration was suspended or conditioned as immediate action` THEN `the Act does not require you to be told of immediate registration action (s 279 requires notice to the practitioner's employers); the decision on your complaint is notified under s 278`
         WHEN `a matter is before QCAT` THEN `yes: you are a person QCAT notices must be given to, and notices the practitioner would give you come from the ombudsman or the National Board (s 95)`
         WHEN `the conciliation of my complaint ended` THEN `yes: a copy of the conciliator's results report, with any agreement reached (s 145(2), (4))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 11 -- conciliation (ss 140, 149)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 140(2) "If the health ombudsman takes immediate action under part 7, conciliation must not start until-- (a) the
-- immediate action ends under section 62 or 73; or (b) the time that conciliation may start under subsection (3) to
-- (5), whichever is relevant to the action taken ... after taking the immediate action." (3) investigation: "until the
-- investigation has been completed and an investigation report has been prepared." (4) referral under part 9: "until
-- the National Agency or other entity has finished dealing with the matter." (5) referral to the director: "until-- (a)
-- the matter ... has been referred to QCAT and QCAT has decided the matter; or (b) the director ... has referred the
-- complaint back to the health ombudsman". (6) "However, despite subsections (2) to (5), the health ombudsman may start
-- conciliation ... at any time if-- (a) the relevant health service provider has agreed to conciliation only for the
-- purpose of arranging a financial settlement or other compensation with the complainant; and (b) the health ombudsman
-- is satisfied the conciliation will not compromise or interfere with the other action ...".
-- s 38(2) (who may have conciliation) is asked separately.
DECLARE `Other action taken` IS ONE OF
    `none of these`
    `immediate action against the practitioner (part 7)`
    `an investigation (part 8)`
    `a referral to Ahpra or another government body (part 9)`
    `a referral to the director of proceedings (part 10)`

DECLARE `When can conciliation start` IS ONE OF
    `s 140 does not hold it back: no action that delays conciliation has been taken`
    `it may start now: the condition s 140 sets for that action has been met`
    `it may start now, but only for arranging a financial settlement or other compensation with you (s 140(6))`
    `not yet: it must wait until the immediate action ends, or the later action's condition is met (s 140(2))`
    `not yet: it must wait until the investigation is completed and its report prepared (s 140(3))`
    `not yet: it must wait until Ahpra or the other body has finished dealing with the matter (s 140(4))`
    `not yet: it must wait until QCAT has decided the matter or the director has referred it back to the ombudsman (s 140(5))`

@export Can conciliation of my complaint (which may lead to a binding settlement or compensation from the provider) start now, given the other action the health ombudsman has taken (s 140)?
GIVEN `the other action` IS A `Other action taken` @desc Which other action has the health ombudsman taken on your complaint?
      `its condition is met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that action reached its end point: the immediate action has ended (or the action taken after it has reached its end point); the investigation is complete and its report prepared; Ahpra or the other body has finished with it; or QCAT has decided it or the director has referred it back?
      `the provider agreed only for a financial settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the provider agreed to conciliation only to arrange a financial settlement or other compensation with you?
      `the ombudsman is satisfied it will not interfere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the ombudsman satisfied that conciliation will not compromise or interfere with the other action?
GIVETH A `When can conciliation start`
`When can conciliation start question` `the other action` `its condition is met` `the provider agreed only for a financial settlement` `the ombudsman is satisfied it will not interfere` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the other action`
    WHEN `none of these` THEN `s 140 does not hold it back: no action that delays conciliation has been taken`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `its condition is met`
        THEN `it may start now: the condition s 140 sets for that action has been met`
        ELSE IF `the provider agreed only for a financial settlement` AND `the ombudsman is satisfied it will not interfere`
             THEN `it may start now, but only for arranging a financial settlement or other compensation with you (s 140(6))`
             ELSE CONSIDER `the other action`
                  WHEN `immediate action against the practitioner (part 7)` THEN `not yet: it must wait until the immediate action ends, or the later action's condition is met (s 140(2))`
                  WHEN `an investigation (part 8)` THEN `not yet: it must wait until the investigation is completed and its report prepared (s 140(3))`
                  WHEN `a referral to Ahpra or another government body (part 9)` THEN `not yet: it must wait until Ahpra or the other body has finished dealing with the matter (s 140(4))`
                  OTHERWISE `not yet: it must wait until QCAT has decided the matter or the director has referred it back to the ombudsman (s 140(5))`

-- s 149(1) "This section applies to-- (a) anything said or admitted during a conciliation (the information); or (b) a
-- document, or a copy of a document, prepared for, or in the course of, a conciliation." (2) "The information, document
-- or copy-- (a) is not admissible as evidence in a proceeding before a court, tribunal or disciplinary body; and (b)
-- can not be used by the health ombudsman as a ground for an investigation or inquiry or as evidence in an
-- investigation or inquiry; and (c) can not be used by the National Agency or a National Board for the purpose of
-- taking health, conduct or performance action under the National Law." (3) "... can not be admitted in a proceeding to
-- enforce a contract mentioned in section 146." (4) "Subsection (2) does not apply to-- (a) the information, if the
-- parties to the conciliation and all persons named in the information consent to its admission or use; or (b) the
-- document or copy, if the person who prepared the document and all persons named in the document consent ...".
DECLARE `What from conciliation` IS ONE OF
    `something said or admitted during the conciliation`
    `a document prepared for or in the course of the conciliation`

DECLARE `Use of it` IS ONE OF
    `as evidence in a court, tribunal or disciplinary body (including a claim for damages or to enforce the settlement)`
    `by the ombudsman as a ground for, or evidence in, an investigation or inquiry`
    `by Ahpra or a National Board for health, conduct or performance action`
    `some other use`

DECLARE `Can it be used` IS ONE OF
    `no: it cannot be used that way (s 149(2))`
    `s 149 does not stop it: everyone whose consent is needed has consented (s 149(4))`
    `s 149 does not deal with that use (a conciliator, and those the conciliator tells, must keep it confidential: s 150)`

@export Can what was said, or a document prepared, in the conciliation of my complaint be used as evidence in court or elsewhere (s 149)?
GIVEN `what it is` IS A `What from conciliation` @desc Is it something said or admitted during the conciliation, or a document prepared for or in it?
      `the use` IS A `Use of it` @desc How would it be used?
      `the consents are given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For something said: have the parties and everyone named in it consented? For a document: have the person who prepared it and everyone named in it consented?
GIVETH A `Can it be used`
`Can what was said in conciliation be used question` `what it is` `the use` `the consents are given` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the use`
    WHEN `some other use` THEN `s 149 does not deal with that use (a conciliator, and those the conciliator tells, must keep it confidential: s 150)`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the consents are given`
        THEN `s 149 does not stop it: everyone whose consent is needed has consented (s 149(4))`
        ELSE `no: it cannot be used that way (s 149(2))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 17 -- protection from reprisal, gagging agreements and liability (ss 261, 263, 263A, 264, 275)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 261(1) "A person must not cause, or attempt or conspire to cause, detriment to another person because, or in the
-- belief that, any person has taken, or intends to take, protected action." (3) "Also, a person must not-- (a) use
-- threats or intimidation to attempt to persuade another person not to take protected action; or (b) dismiss, or
-- refuse to employ, another person because, or in the belief that, the other person has taken, or intends to take,
-- protected action." (6) "... it is sufficient if the unlawful ground is a substantial ground ...". (7) "protected
-- action means-- (a) making either of the following, in good faith-- (i) a health service complaint; (ii) a
-- notification under the National Law ...; or (b) giving information, documents or other assistance in the course of
-- an investigation or for another purpose under this Act or the National Law ...". s 262 offence: "(a) for an
-- individual--375 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment; or (b) for a corporation--750 penalty units." s 263(1) "A
-- reprisal is a tort and a person who takes a reprisal is liable in damages to any person who suffers detriment as a
-- result." (3) a claim that goes to trial in the Supreme or District Court is decided by a judge without a jury.
DECLARE `The protected action` IS ONE OF
    `a health service complaint or a National Law notification made in good faith`
    `a complaint or notification not made in good faith`
    `giving information, documents or other help to an investigation or for another purpose under this Act or the National Law`
    `none`

DECLARE `What was done` IS ONE OF
    `detriment caused, or attempted or conspired at`
    `threats or intimidation to persuade someone not to take protected action`
    `dismissal or refusal to employ`
    `nothing of these kinds`

DECLARE `Is it a reprisal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a reprisal; it is a tort and you may claim damages for the detriment you suffered (tried without a jury in the Supreme or District Court), and it is an offence (up to 375 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment for an individual, 750 penalty units for a corporation) (ss 261-263)`
    `yes: a reprisal and an offence (ss 261, 262); damages under s 263 go to a person who suffers detriment as a result, which you say you have not`
    `no: the action was not protected action (complaints and notifications must be made in good faith) (s 261(7))`
    `no: s 261 covers detriment, threats or intimidation, and dismissal or refusal to employ`
    `not shown: s 261(6) makes a substantial ground enough; the Act does not say whether a lesser ground is`

@export Was what was done to me for complaining (or helping an investigation) a reprisal, and can I sue for damages (ss 261-263)?
GIVEN `the protected action` IS A `The protected action` @desc What had you (or another person) done, or intended to do, or was believed to have done?
      `what was done` IS A `What was done` @desc What was done to you?
      `it was a substantial reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that action, or the belief about it, a substantial reason for what was done (it need not be the only one)? For threats, answer yes.
      `I suffered detriment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you suffer detriment (loss, harm, disadvantage) as a result?
GIVETH A `Is it a reprisal`
`Can I sue for a reprisal question` `the protected action` `what was done` `it was a substantial reason` `I suffered detriment` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the protected action`
    WHEN `a complaint or notification not made in good faith` THEN `no: the action was not protected action (complaints and notifications must be made in good faith) (s 261(7))`
    WHEN `none` THEN `no: the action was not protected action (complaints and notifications must be made in good faith) (s 261(7))`
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `what was done`
        WHEN `nothing of these kinds` THEN `no: s 261 covers detriment, threats or intimidation, and dismissal or refusal to employ`
        OTHERWISE
            IF (NOT `it was a substantial reason`)
            THEN `not shown: s 261(6) makes a substantial ground enough; the Act does not say whether a lesser ground is`
            ELSE IF `I suffered detriment`
                 THEN `yes: a reprisal; it is a tort and you may claim damages for the detriment you suffered (tried without a jury in the Supreme or District Court), and it is an offence (up to 375 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment for an individual, 750 penalty units for a corporation) (ss 261-263)`
                 ELSE `yes: a reprisal and an offence (ss 261, 262); damages under s 263 go to a person who suffers detriment as a result, which you say you have not`

-- s 263A(1) "A provision of a non-disclosure agreement is void to the extent it prevents or limits a person from-- (a)
-- making ..., in good faith-- (i) a health service complaint; (ii) a notification under the National Law ...; or (b)
-- giving information, documents or other assistance ...". (2) "A relevant person must not enter into a non-disclosure
-- agreement unless the agreement sets out, clearly and in writing, that the agreement does not limit a person from--
-- [the same]. Maximum penalty-- (a) for an individual--30 penalty units; or (b) for a corporation--60 penalty units."
-- (3) "non-disclosure agreement means a contract or other agreement that prohibits or restricts the disclosure of
-- information or documents by a person in relation to the health, conduct or performance of a health service provider
-- or former health service provider." "relevant person means ... (a) an employer or former employer of a health
-- service provider or former health service provider; (b) a health service provider."
DECLARE `About my agreement` IS ONE OF
    `the agreement is not a non-disclosure agreement under s 263A (it does not restrict disclosure about a health service provider's health, conduct or performance)`
    `the provision is void to the extent it stops or limits you making a good-faith complaint or notification, or helping the ombudsman or Ahpra (s 263A(1))`
    `the other party, an employer or health service provider, entered it without stating clearly in writing that it does not limit complaints, notifications or help: an offence (up to 30 penalty units for an individual, 60 for a corporation) (s 263A(2))`
    `nothing in s 263A affects it on these facts`

@export I signed an agreement (for example a settlement or employment agreement) that restricts what I can say about a health practitioner or health service: is it void as far as it stops me complaining, and did the other party break the law by making it (s 263A)?
GIVEN `it restricts disclosure about a provider's health, conduct or performance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the agreement prohibit or restrict you disclosing information or documents about the health, conduct or performance of a health service provider (current or former)?
      `it stops or limits complaints or help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a provision of it stop or limit you making a complaint or notification in good faith, or giving information or help to the health ombudsman, Ahpra or an investigation?
      `the other party is an employer or provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the other party a health service provider, or an employer (or former employer) of one?
      `it states clearly in writing that complaints are not limited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the agreement set out clearly, in writing, that it does not limit you complaining, notifying or helping?
GIVETH A LIST OF `About my agreement`
`Is my non-disclosure agreement void question` `it restricts disclosure about a provider's health, conduct or performance` `it stops or limits complaints or help` `the other party is an employer or provider` `it states clearly in writing that complaints are not limited` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it restricts disclosure about a provider's health, conduct or performance`)
    THEN LIST `the agreement is not a non-disclosure agreement under s 263A (it does not restrict disclosure about a health service provider's health, conduct or performance)`
    ELSE IF `the void part` EQUALS EMPTY AND `the offence part` EQUALS EMPTY
         THEN LIST `nothing in s 263A affects it on these facts`
         ELSE concat (LIST `the void part`, `the offence part`)
    WHERE
        `the void part` MEANS
            IF `it stops or limits complaints or help`
            THEN LIST `the provision is void to the extent it stops or limits you making a good-faith complaint or notification, or helping the ombudsman or Ahpra (s 263A(1))`
            ELSE EMPTY
        `the offence part` MEANS
            IF `the other party is an employer or provider` AND (NOT `it states clearly in writing that complaints are not limited`)
            THEN LIST `the other party, an employer or health service provider, entered it without stating clearly in writing that it does not limit complaints, notifications or help: an offence (up to 30 penalty units for an individual, 60 for a corporation) (s 263A(2))`
            ELSE EMPTY

-- s 275(1) "This section applies to a person who, honestly and on reasonable grounds, gives information to the health
-- ombudsman, a staff member of the Office of the Health Ombudsman or an authorised person-- (a) for the purpose of a
-- health service complaint; or (b) in the course of an investigation ... or inquiry ...; or (c) otherwise under this
-- Act." (2) "The person is not subject to any liability for giving the information and no action, claim or demand may
-- be taken or made of or against the person for giving the information." (3) no breach of professional etiquette or
-- ethics; (4)(a) "in a proceeding for defamation, the person has a defence of absolute privilege"; (4)(b) no breach of
-- a duty of confidentiality. s 264(1) knowingly false or misleading information: maximum penalty 100 penalty units.
-- "Honestly and on reasonable grounds" is an application condition: outside it, s 275 gives no protection on these
-- facts (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)).
DECLARE `Am I protected` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you cannot be sued or made liable for giving it, you have absolute privilege against defamation, and you breach no duty of confidence or professional ethics by giving it (s 275)`
    `no: s 275 gives no protection on these facts, and giving information you know is false or misleading is an offence (up to 100 penalty units) (s 264)`
    `no: s 275 gives no protection on these facts; it protects only information given honestly and on reasonable grounds`
    `no: s 275 protects only information given to the health ombudsman, its staff or an authorised person`

@export Am I protected from being sued (including for defamation) or disciplined for giving information to the health ombudsman about my complaint (s 275)?
GIVEN `I gave it to the ombudsman, its staff or an authorised person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you give the information to the health ombudsman, a staff member of the Office of the Health Ombudsman, or an authorised person?
      `I knew it was false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know the information was false or misleading in a material way?
      `I gave it honestly and on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you give it honestly and on reasonable grounds (whether the grounds were reasonable is for a court)?
GIVETH A `Am I protected`
`Am I protected for giving information question` `I gave it to the ombudsman, its staff or an authorised person` `I knew it was false or misleading` `I gave it honestly and on reasonable grounds` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I gave it to the ombudsman, its staff or an authorised person`)
    THEN `no: s 275 protects only information given to the health ombudsman, its staff or an authorised person`
    ELSE IF `I knew it was false or misleading`
         THEN `no: s 275 gives no protection on these facts, and giving information you know is false or misleading is an offence (up to 100 penalty units) (s 264)`
         ELSE IF `I gave it honestly and on reasonable grounds`
              THEN `yes: you cannot be sued or made liable for giving it, you have absolute privilege against defamation, and you breach no duty of confidence or professional ethics by giving it (s 275)`
              ELSE `no: s 275 gives no protection on these facts; it protects only information given honestly and on reasonable grounds`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 15 division 6 -- compensation for loss from an authorised person's powers (s 232)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 232(1) "A person may claim compensation from the State if the person incurs loss because of the exercise, or
-- purported exercise, of a power by or for an authorised person including a loss arising from compliance with a
-- requirement made of the person under division 3 or 5." (2) "However, subsection (1) does not include loss arising
-- from a lawful seizure or a lawful forfeiture." (3) claimed in a court with jurisdiction for the amount, or in a
-- proceeding for the alleged offence; (4) "A court may order the payment of compensation only if it is satisfied it is
-- just ..."; (5) the court "must have regard to any relevant offence committed by the claimant." (8) "loss includes
-- costs and damage."
DECLARE `Can I claim compensation` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may claim compensation (including costs and damage) from the State in a court; it may order payment only if satisfied it is just, having regard to any relevant offence you committed (s 232)`
    `no: loss from a lawful seizure or lawful forfeiture is excluded (s 232(2))`
    `no: s 232 covers only loss caused by an authorised person's exercise (or purported exercise) of a power, or by complying with a requirement they made`

@export Can I claim compensation from the State for loss caused when an authorised person of the health ombudsman entered, searched, seized or required information (s 232)?
GIVEN `the loss came from an authorised person's power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your loss (including costs and damage) come from an authorised person (or someone acting for one) exercising or purporting to exercise a power, or from complying with a requirement they made of you?
      `it came from a lawful seizure or forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the loss come from a lawful seizure or lawful forfeiture of a thing?
GIVETH A `Can I claim compensation`
`Can I claim compensation from the State question` `the loss came from an authorised person's power` `it came from a lawful seizure or forfeiture` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the loss came from an authorised person's power`)
    THEN `no: s 232 covers only loss caused by an authorised person's exercise (or purported exercise) of a power, or by complying with a requirement they made`
    ELSE IF `it came from a lawful seizure or forfeiture`
         THEN `no: loss from a lawful seizure or lawful forfeiture is excluded (s 232(2))`
         ELSE `yes: you may claim compensation (including costs and damage) from the State in a court; it may order payment only if satisfied it is just, having regard to any relevant offence you committed (s 232)`
